WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.602
Memory safety starts with one practical
question:

2
00:00:05.682 --> 00:00:13.870
what object does this pointer refer to,
and how long does that object remain valid.

3
00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:17.391
In C, a pointer carries an address,

4
00:00:17.471 --> 00:00:23.523
but it does not carry a complete runtime
ownership contract.

5
00:00:23.603 --> 00:00:31.079
The code and its surrounding conventions
must make that contract visible.

6
00:00:31.159 --> 00:00:36.261
Stack objects, heap allocations, static
storage,

7
00:00:36.341 --> 00:00:42.631
and borrowed pointers have different
lifetime expectations.

8
00:00:42.711 --> 00:00:49.356
Stack objects usually stop being valid
when the function returns.

9
00:00:49.436 --> 00:00:54.182
Heap allocations remain valid until
released.

10
00:00:54.262 --> 00:00:59.009
Static storage lasts for the program
lifetime.

11
00:00:59.089 --> 00:01:07.276
Borrowed pointers may be valid only while
another owner preserves the underlying

12
00:01:07.356 --> 00:01:08.187
object.

13
00:01:08.267 --> 00:01:16.336
Every dynamically allocated object should
have a clear owner and a clear cleanup

14
00:01:16.416 --> 00:01:17.009
path.

15
00:01:17.089 --> 00:01:23.853
Ownership can belong to a function, a
structure, a module, a caller,

16
00:01:23.933 --> 00:01:29.510
or a transfer operation, but it should not
be ambiguous.

17
00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:35.879
Ambiguity is where leaks, double cleanup,
dangling pointers,

18
00:01:35.959 --> 00:01:39.163
and invalid access often begin.

19
00:01:39.243 --> 00:01:43.990
Initialize variables and pointers before
use.

20
00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:52.020
Uninitialized data can carry whatever
value happened to be present in storage.

21
00:01:52.100 --> 00:02:00.407
That creates unpredictable behavior and
makes review harder because the code does

22
00:02:00.487 --> 00:02:04.047
not show a reliable starting state.

23
00:02:04.127 --> 00:02:11.246
Clear initialization also makes cleanup
paths easier to write safely.

24
00:02:11.326 --> 00:02:13.581
After memory is freed,

25
00:02:13.661 --> 00:02:20.425
avoid continuing to use aliases that still
point to the old storage.

26
00:02:20.505 --> 00:02:27.506
Setting one pointer to null does not
update every copy of that pointer.

27
00:02:27.586 --> 00:02:35.180
Code review should look for stored
aliases, callbacks, global references,

28
00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:42.380
and structures that may retain a pointer
beyond the object's lifetime.

29
00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:50.648
Avoid returning pointers to stack-local
data because the object will no longer be

30
00:02:50.728 --> 00:02:54.169
valid after the function returns.

31
00:02:54.249 --> 00:02:59.351
Prefer small scopes, visible ownership
comments,

32
00:02:59.431 --> 00:03:05.602
and cleanup patterns that make lifetime
rules easy to follow.

33
00:03:05.682 --> 00:03:09.835
The aim is not decorative documentation;

34
00:03:09.915 --> 00:03:18.340
it is making memory ownership clear enough
that the next maintainer can preserve it.
